   "l": "By the straight, govern a state;\nby the odd, use arms;\nby having no business, take all under heaven.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Govern a state by what is straight; use arms by what is unexpected; take all under heaven by having no business at all.",
   "n": "Three clauses, each opening with 以 'by means of' and each naming its own instrument — and the third breaks the pattern the first two have just set up. Rule and surprise are both things you do; 無事 is the absence of them, and it is what wins the whole.\n\n奇 is a word out of the art of war: the 正 is the force drawn up where it should be, the 奇 the one that comes from where it should not. Chapter 58 will make the two turn into each other.\n\n取天下 was chapter 48's line too — 取天下常以無事, 'the taking of all under heaven is always by having no business'.",
   "p": true
  },
