   ],
   "l": "By what do I know that it is so?\nBy this.\nAll under heaven, many taboos —\nand the people ever the poorer;\nthe people, many sharp implements —\nand realm and household the more benighted;",
   "v": true,
   "i": "How do I know that this is so? By what follows. The more taboos there are in all under heaven, the poorer the people; the more sharp instruments the people have, the darker the realm grows;",
   "n": "何以 is 以何 with the question word pulled to the front — the fixed rule of the language, and the same formula that closed chapters 21 and 54. Here 'by this' points forward rather than back: what proves it is the four couplets that follow.\n\nThose couplets have no correlative pair, no 'the more … the more'. Each carries 彌 or 滋, 'increasingly', in its second clause only; nothing whatever marks the first clause as the condition, and the correlation is left for you to make out of the parallel. This is the language at its most compressed: 天下多忌諱 is five characters — 'all-under-heaven — many — taboos'.\n\n昬 is the ordinary 昏 'dark' written with a variant top; chapter 18 prints the plain form of the same word, 國家昏亂, of a realm falling into disorder. The state does not merely weaken, it goes dark."
  },
