   "l": "Therefore the sage says:\n'I act without forcing, and the people transform of themselves;\nI love stillness, and the people set themselves straight;\nI have no business, and the people grow rich of themselves;\nI am without desire, and the people are of themselves the uncarved block.'",
   "v": true,
   "i": "So the sage says: I act without forcing, and the people transform themselves; I love stillness, and the people straighten themselves; I go about no business, and the people grow rich of themselves; I want nothing, and the people become of themselves the uncarved block.",
   "n": "自 is the pivot of all four lines — 'of themselves', standing before its verb, never after. Nothing is done to the people in any of them: the ruler's half of each line is a negative or a stillness, and the people's half is the only place where anything happens.\n\n正 comes round for the third time in this chapter, and now it is a verb the people do to themselves. The ruler who governed 以正 in the first line has stopped governing at all.\n\n樸, the uncarved block, is used here as a verb — 'they of-themselves uncarved-block' — which no English will do. It is the state of timber that has not been worked; 'plain' or 'simple' would lose the wood.\n\nThe four clauses are quoted as a saying, and the older manuscripts give them in a different order. Nothing in the sense turns on it."
